      "lineNbr": 6530,
      "author": {
        "id": 1981633
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-02-09T16:46:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "@gprocida@google.com @tkjos@google.com @jstultz@google.com\n\nI believe this is how the fuse is implemented. The default value is 0, and the extra1 field is the \u0027minimum\u0027 value that can be passed, and the extra2 field is the max. Therefore it can only be set to 1. \n\nPerhaps a comment could be added to these lines explaining this.\n\nThat being said, there was some discussion of how this is used, and weather or not we want it to be a one-time fuse. If we leave it as a fuse, we will need logic in the networking module to not set this in -eng or -userdebug builds to allow for bpftrace and debugging bpf programs.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6529,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 6530,
        "endChar": 24
      },
      "fixSuggestions": [
        {
          "fixId": "732101fd_2d8cfd3a",
          "description": "prompt_to_edit API",
          "replacements": [
            {
              "path": "kernel/bpf/syscall.c",
              "range": {
                "startLine": 6529,
                "startChar": 0,
                "endLine": 6529,
                "endChar": 0
              },
              "replacement": "\t\t/* This sysctl acts as a one-time fuse. Both extra1 (min) and\n\t\t * extra2 (max) are set to 1, meaning the only value that can\n\t\t * be written is 1. The default value is 0.\n\t\t */\n"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "revId": "68e69281c5211db350fdcd9942d91fab14266d67",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "90314b3a_f6c5947b",
        "filename": "kernel/bpf/syscall.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 6530,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003247
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-02-09T19:12:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "All testing happens on userdebug builds...\nso that has to mirror user build behaviour.\n\nWe certainly could leave it (ie. not flip the fuse) alone on eng builds....\n\n---\n\nWe could introduce some property that would prevent flipping the fuse on userdebug builds.\n\n---\n\nAlternatively we could try to make this a tristate of some sort:\n0 - bpf_prog_load enabled (boot time default)\n1 - bpf_prog_load disabled \u0026 can be re-enabled (set on userdebug/eng)\n2 - bpf_prog_load disabled \u0026 cannot be re-enabled (set on user)\n\nHowever, that requires a custom kernel sysctl write handler...\n[there\u0027s no way to implement the above with just min/max limits]\n\n---\n\nAlternatively a much cheaper to implement solution would be to have *two* sysctls, both with the same blocks-load behaviour, but one has min/max\u003d0/1 and can thus be re-enabled, while the other has min/max\u003d1/1 and is thus a fuse.\n\nWe\u0027d flip one on userdebug/eng, and the fuse on user builds.\n\nThis would roughly double the size of this patch, but is conceptually trivial...\n\nie.\nif (sysctl_bpf_disable_prog_load || sysctl_bpf_disable_prog_load_fuse)\n\t\treturn -EUNATCH;\n\n---\n\nThoughts?",
      "parentUuid": "3e260945_2a3ad19e",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6529,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 6530,
        "endChar": 24
      },
      "revId": "68e69281c5211db350fdcd9942d91fab14266d67",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "529fa940_4671d335",
